#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e89e99e-07af-5529-a0c3-3af7fe4158cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-157256" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =4\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement “The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent” is false. Since statement “${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$” is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $4{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=0.$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275389 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca63685-40ca-5dbf-8721-4e6b3a5a2913" :question/math-academy-id "q-157290" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $2y=3x$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $2y=3x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =3x \\\\ 2(0) & =3(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a+\\frac{3}{2}b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(\\frac{3}{2}a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $2y=3x.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "712144d5-1b91-55e7-970d-beb256e92d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-157300" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(2,1,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2b \\\\ b \\\\ -b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2a+2b \\\\ a+b \\\\ (-a)+(-b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(a+b) \\\\ a+b \\\\ -(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2a \\\\ a \\\\ -a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(2a) \\\\ ka \\\\ k(-a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2(ka) \\\\ ka \\\\ -(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{2}=y=-z.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$L$ contains the zero-vector”; “$L$ is closed under vector addition”; “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f036c2d4-285c-5c5e-a300-6732346f17c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157374" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275392 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fec918c0-b48b-51d4-9fda-9d6b7c9058ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-157375" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,3,-2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4b \\\\ 3b \\\\ -2b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4a+4b \\\\ 3a+3b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b) \\\\ -2(a+b)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4a \\\\ 3a \\\\ -2a\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k(4a) \\\\ k(3a) \\\\ k(-2a)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4(ka) \\\\ 3(ka) \\\\ -2(ka)\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=-\\frac{z}{2}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84592f87-8902-5e5c-972a-3aba1b1c18c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-157377" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,1,1)$ and $(2,-1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=z-1.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (0)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(3,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(3)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (3)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275394 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21a6de96-a543-5af1-a71d-fa101cbb68c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-157392" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,-1,1)$ and $(3,0,-1).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$L$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=y=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n\\frac{(0)-3}{2}\\ne (0)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider the vectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(4,-1,0)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n\\frac{(4)-3}{2}\\ne (-1)\\ne \\frac{(0)+1}{-2}.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$L$ is closed under vector addition” and “$L$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42d6a94c-6a33-5351-adc5-bba146953de0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157463" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=2x-y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 2x-y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=2x-y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2x-y \\\\ (0) & =2(0)-(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ 2{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ (2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})+(2{b}_{1}-{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 2({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})-({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ 2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k(2{a}_{1}-{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ 2(k{a}_{1})-k{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=2x-y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Π$ contains the zero-vector”; “$Π$ is closed under vector addition”; “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19a5b6b6-a775-5cf8-81ee-dfec54297bbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-157464" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $z=x+2y$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ x+2y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $z=x+2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x+2y \\\\ (0) & =(0)+2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+2{b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+2{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1}+{b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}+{b}_{2} \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1})+2({a}_{2}+{b}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Let, for example, $a=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k({a}_{1}+2{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k{a}_{1} \\\\ k{a}_{2} \\\\ k{a}_{1}+2(k{a}_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=x+2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in Π.$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are true, the plane $Π$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70407f65-cb23-502b-8caa-ecb6f34e8d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-157468" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x+y-2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3-y+2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (0)+(0)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2 \\\\ 0+1 \\\\ 0+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-2z & =3 \\\\ (5)+(1)-2(0) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "39240080-5310-56c6-b798-6051cae1cac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-157469" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2y-z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1+0 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+z & =1 \\\\ (1)-2(0)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is true. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99eda82b-48bc-5827-9c44-f2725d46a5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157476" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275420 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ecd1a30-d61f-5d31-96d2-62fa52545c5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158029" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot \\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0609c9a8-63cb-5946-854d-790ce4ad14b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-158100" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ or ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-158101" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" :question/math-academy-id "q-158102" :question/problem "Which of the diagrams shown below represent subspaces of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $\\mathbf{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\hspace{-0.167em},\\hspace{0.167em}$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275444 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "210e9083-358f-508e-9376-e356564e0e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158104" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 7 & 13 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|24|=24.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275445 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edcd069d-5898-5a9f-9b4f-3515e782b5c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-158106" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 8 & -11 & -2\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-2)=-72.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-72|=72.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275446 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32e6e8c1-9b26-5825-ac5f-18dfd683a674" :question/math-academy-id "q-158108" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=7\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-105.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-105|=105.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275447 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a0a4e7-70bf-5d4f-8473-1e3ebaad90ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-158109" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 7 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=(-3)\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|180|=180.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275448 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4e39b2b-6ddb-522a-a369-dfb16a50311d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158110" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 12 & 5\\end{bmatrix}=6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 5=-180.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-180|=180.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275449 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3a8456a-5f67-54bf-8d0a-07a8bbe82bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158114" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{3}} & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{-2}} & 1 & 5 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275468 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c594e512-d19a-53c5-8f50-4caaf295111c" :question/math-academy-id "q-158368" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 5 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $3$rd column of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 1 \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{1}} & {\\color{lightgray}{4}} & 3 & {\\color{lightgray}{6}} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-120|=120.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275469 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d7bd54b-d293-5ee2-b790-9af4e0cb0ced" :question/math-academy-id "q-158369" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot 5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3=-45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-45|=45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275470 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94fb05dd-7b6c-5fef-becd-0abc3ccf0dc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158371" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}:$\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $1$st row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 2 & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} \\\\ -2 & {\\color{lightgray}{7}} & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & {\\color{lightgray}{5}} & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 & {\\color{lightgray}{3}} \\\\ {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & {\\color{lightgray}{0}} & 4 \\\\ -3 & 7 & {\\color{lightgray}{2}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 4\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(-14+30) \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|128|=128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278261 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ffee0ea-a323-5b51-850e-98d4c4b547d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159784" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$” is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=4$ is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ (not $4$).\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=3$ is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}})=2$ (not $3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w}$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284752 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "044de50e-6da8-5a08-93e7-4cbfebd23610" :question/math-academy-id "q-177658" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is a square matrix” is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix” is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is a square matrix” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284753 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d52fcd7c-0cb8-5f3b-83e6-5d87eafb229f" :question/math-academy-id "q-177660" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is a square matrix” is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix” is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is a square matrix”; $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$; “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284754 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c17d5c10-3940-55d7-92e1-ac427e80f858" :question/math-academy-id "q-177662" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is a square matrix” is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix” is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is a square matrix”; $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$; “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix”."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284755 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ef1bd48-9355-501c-9dc0-0c7250b226e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-177663" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is a square matrix” is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$ is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 21 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix” is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is a square matrix” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284756 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186668820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71c46d34-a9b9-54b7-8de2-8c7deffeb5f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-177667" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement “$A$ is a square matrix” is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$ is true. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an orthogonal matrix” is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix, our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287677 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91c44b7d-e168-56b9-b4bf-b877a54ec61d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181130" :question/problem "![](images/37/37294502ac1c3c15790f5b820b2499fd03c16edb53b2faa90080507f3065eb84.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside or on the rectangle's edges if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1\\le x\\le 5,\\hspace{2em}1\\le y\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287718 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b635e983-fa39-5831-8b68-a64de13e9b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-181679" :question/problem "![](images/71/718ee39f4a0b2e9cca18ed4de77271a90139b13e8b71990611c1e13357245713.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+5,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+3\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=-x+5$ and *below* the line $y=x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-x+5,\\hspace{2em}y<x+3.\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-x+5,\\hspace{1em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x+3\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287719 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f795bd-05e4-56c1-a78c-5dcc5d6493ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-181682" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1d71eb496878cedda9defd9932e3bc42ecb2772ad06e298e06881d1fb51f184.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-2\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=-2$ and $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}\\frac{1}{2}x+2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287720 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca50087b-8402-59b5-bb30-6e666b7fb11c" :question/math-academy-id "q-181684" :question/problem "![](images/09/09df7202fbe0c9160e6c10dc10a813bc6b4b00aae7d95a01068dd4154fb5d929.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-2\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<x.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<x\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287721 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2898c964-611b-56ce-8ed9-635cb443e151" :question/math-academy-id "q-181685" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2d1c647ec7899221567d67e24e90a9f1efca60650b82a739e843e4085be636e.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,2x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,-x+3\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2x$ and *below* the line $y=-x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2x,\\hspace{2em}y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n2x<y<-x+3.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-x+3\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287722 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db5f24a8-6bb9-526d-89dd-0ad32386f5f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-181686" :question/problem "![](images/67/67eeabfcff4eb24e14c15691c1c9de746a8870808197739577f7696faeada5bc.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,4,\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,4x+1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=4$ and *below* the line $y=4x+1.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<4,\\hspace{2em}y<4x+1.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=4$ and $y=4x+1$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4,\\hspace{2em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4x+1\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4,\\hspace{1em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}4x+1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287723 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c15957e5-b917-5445-9850-6ff678f7a755" :question/math-academy-id "q-181687" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6a3b3bc0064525271804e6d20689c21ca84c0adad138a8bd8d398be66e45c3e.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-x-1\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,x+2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1$ and *below* the line $y=x+2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1,\\hspace{2em}y<x+2.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x-1<y<x+2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the lines are dashed), the inequalities are strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-x-1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x+2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287724 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287464}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dab3d6dc-4b31-53f2-8ab3-bc64485c9198" :question/math-academy-id "q-181688" :question/problem "![](images/46/4628a6e45445a585361f7d32586b27e29bc726d4ed8b7f072679e5537df59644.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x+1\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,3x\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=x+1$ and *below* the line $y=3x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>x+1,\\hspace{2em}y<3x.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\nx+1<y<3x.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx+1<y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}3x\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x+1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}3x\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287725 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281f8e94-72c2-5b73-8bcb-5cb4814a0f37" :question/math-academy-id "q-181689" :question/problem "![](images/32/32168a2cb1fe14dbc88725347611ae5951198e81dfaa0b41e780106750bbb741.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,3x\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,\\frac{x}{4}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3x$ and *below* the line $y=\\frac{x}{4}.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3x,\\hspace{2em}y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3x<y<\\frac{x}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n3x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<\\frac{x}{4}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}\\frac{x}{4}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287726 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6ec6a93c-1a73-5b19-bbef-75f3b804c771" :question/math-academy-id "q-181690" :question/problem "![](images/88/883d270ae063f134ee0ac04f717bcc9b5800adb939be3c7f19f25602ad52360e.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,-2\\,{{field-2}}\\,y\\,{{field-3}}\\,2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-2$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-2,\\hspace{2em}y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-2<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-2<y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287727 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e71b9628-b280-5047-9c68-527f1ea3edca" :question/math-academy-id "q-181691" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6affe267a93d1dab16de3dc153fddbdb165bab831790f7387dcbd29354ebb63a.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287728 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2547003-ad79-57a3-97bf-c8a4091bfc89" :question/math-academy-id "q-181692" :question/problem "![](images/70/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287729 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1cd9f8d0-368b-57f9-99c0-0235eafbffc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181694" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c670ed592fc32716596aae470911923627d1c6861a75a1c885659d09005e0a84.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em\\}$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$36$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+4(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}<}\\hspace{0.222em}36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}9}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}36\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287730 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "644c8b7b-2ed7-59bb-b325-c2361f722e5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181696" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cd989164ddda8a542098548f401a055e085943e35d95a63fe47b132f723d8ce.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em\\}$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$64$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=2$ and vertical radius $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the ellipse, say, $(0,5).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n16(0)^{2}+4(5)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}>}\\hspace{0.222em}64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}16}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287760 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80918b0e-9572-56bb-b137-be14e694fe5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181791" :question/problem "![](images/af/af3a9e323fa460b60bfa900c7edbcceb4057c442803160b2b4b5bd0704b4db7c.png)\n\nDescribe the set of points shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}},\\,{{field-4}}\\le y\\le {{field-5}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in \\{-1,0,1,2\\},\\hspace{1em}y\\in \\{-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-1\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{1em}-2\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-2}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287765 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49eb2820-ef81-5aa5-ac35-475ea920738e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181804" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a87b00f9f7908fe62542ef7bca94277f040af97b4af24698dd648c167bcd3376.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,5,\\,2\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,5\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\hspace{2em}2<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the bottom edge ($y=2$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1<x<5,\\hspace{2em}2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}5,\\hspace{0.222em}2\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}5\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287766 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c447c7d2-83cd-503b-b04b-0c9b2bcb0661" :question/math-academy-id "q-181806" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a659e2d14a645036488833cd920e6bb3e3d7a7716724dba63814be6df4066fe9.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,-1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\hspace{2em}-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x<6,\\hspace{2em}-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}6,\\hspace{0.222em}-1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}4\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287767 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287390}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fc98003-bd40-5665-b2e9-cfd241ea5aff" :question/math-academy-id "q-181807" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c23fe5aa17b46293ff29cd8621ee10b2e143cb23b77811ddf20037a1639bce0.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the left* of the curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx<{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is not included in the set (the curve is dashed), the inequality is strict.\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287768 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f80556dc-02e0-555b-8ef8-6bae1cda35bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-181808" :question/problem "![](images/66/664c70223ca8a6219b04a287c779d20bf0f88d770920d1357303721574d90787.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,{x}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}{x}^{2}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287769 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287215} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "706b2b43-7315-5cd0-9807-cc687006ade4" :question/math-academy-id "q-181809" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8be62d80165b756aedf31e998d6636d7f77a5606e195b3ec288cb0ac6fb52d6f.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em\\}$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}+\\hspace{0.222em}$\n{{field-2}}\n${y}^{2}$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$144$\n\n$\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=144.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the ellipse, say, $(0,4).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n9(0)^{2}+16(4)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}>}\\hspace{0.222em}144.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>144.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $>$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}9}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}16}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}144\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287779 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adce9191-abcc-50fc-a56f-a2f330169645" :question/math-academy-id "q-181970" :question/problem "![](images/01/010d4befaa2bd2142cf63010f79f071df331bb6394eb65e4f883752cb04a2cb4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-x+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-x+1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287781 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "827675a7-728a-52ab-b17b-7e94ef593363" :question/math-academy-id "q-181976" :question/problem "![](images/11/11ec43034f97e66840da012f3beaf2d933fb03cfbe69f510ca5b68f1fd861f83.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x+1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x+1.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x+1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}-x+1.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}-x+1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287785 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287528} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfc3e0f0-9ab6-551c-8d47-c8fca0b2295d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181988" :question/problem "![](images/34/34f5bb40fdcd4f71967ef2b1cc15328cf8d4d02cdd54b19a406273fae6c88ad4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x-1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}-x-1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287786 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53ac5dfb-f75e-5ef2-a84c-e58da3b6698e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181999" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f7b1dc9bf4da61b573345aaf92fe0d29ea00ef5558a9fca985d8af25fa0a03.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}2.\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}>}}2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287790 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49192157-aea0-5727-927f-dcfccbbb5607" :question/math-academy-id "q-182013" :question/problem "![](images/77/7703b8c621b268a9fcbb412cd3a0617045145d475024dc91cecfa09a18fc8712.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The points in our set include all those that lie *right* the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-2\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $x=-2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287792 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0067ce51-137c-57cd-8c4c-7e69e7b8398e" :question/math-academy-id "q-182016" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f54c771d1b7045bbaa301c9d595a45dc64fbdfc6b1c83c152fb6fc892373ea06.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287797 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32fc86ab-aae0-54f7-aa10-d5b80cc4979e" :question/math-academy-id "q-182024" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5adfe417126264290b9b49776a535e8ca27521a6bb13806df13bffe2807402fe.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-1\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* the line $x=-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}-1\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287798 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8779b713-2ad1-53d1-a3e6-394f41bc52c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-182032" :question/problem "![](images/98/980ae73286ed41ed0c6f074a4bed5b35fa852dd608fb0a1a561f26a7eac60ea7.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,2\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *to the left* the line $x=2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx<2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\nx\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287822 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2a99499-e25e-5c3a-b8fd-38e4eb5f4e8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18259" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,3,1⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,4,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3((-1)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 3)+(2\\cdot 4-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =39-6+8 \\\\  & =41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|41|=41.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287823 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9713970-1739-5ce7-ae00-abfb91e16a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-18266" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors  $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,-1,-{k}^{2}⟩,\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,4,8⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 & -{k}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+(-{k}^{2})\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0+(2\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 8)-{k}^{2}(2\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =18-2{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 18-2{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{k}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =9 \\\\ k & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-3$ or $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287826 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc94d59e-a3d1-5387-8338-9ada65b5d11f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18281" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}A=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-4\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(0+9)-4(0-6)+1(0+2) \\\\  & =18+24+2 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{44}\\cdot 6=\\frac{3}{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287827 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b297987-b62d-5714-afb9-ac77feefadc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18282" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-3)=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287828 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9f46698-54a7-56c6-b512-47750a5ae9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18283" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-4.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =15-(-1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{-4}\\cdot 16=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287829 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "670b2139-910a-54c1-b1cc-9c11eb5fdc1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18284" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $-16.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{22}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{22}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{22} & =(-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{22}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{22}=\\frac{1}{-16}\\cdot 4=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287830 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5cade28-be41-57ee-910f-46e652127d0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-18285" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-0+(0-2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{31}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{13} & =(-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{31}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}=\\frac{1}{-2}\\cdot (-2)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287831 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dcb03ff4-bc8a-5928-846f-20e56388fdb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-18286" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $39.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-1-0) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23}=\\frac{1}{39}\\cdot 1=\\frac{1}{39}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287832 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35c43087-68b2-5ef3-b590-6196109cdd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-18287" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $24.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =24-0 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31}=\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot 24=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287833 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b862f7e3-dfeb-537e-b5d6-1de6a752faf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-18288" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix $A$ $=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ if its determinant is $71.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we want the entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{12} & =(-1)^{1+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-3-10) \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{21}^{′}=\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}=\\frac{1}{71}\\cdot 13=\\frac{13}{71}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287954 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5071b5db-b2c7-5b4f-b394-920acc893dc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19764" :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}$, if $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}=8\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{a}+4\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}+4(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}+4(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =(8\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})+4(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =8\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287955 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eed27f20-b67a-5b7d-a3c2-a07a46e7e902" :question/math-academy-id "q-19767" :question/problem "Find $(3\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})$, if $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}=6\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=-3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n(3\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})=(3\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}) & =(3\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-(5\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) \\\\  & =6(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =6(6\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j})-10(-3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =36\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}+30\\mathbf{i}+20\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =66\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287956 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3e9b2b8-80c6-5b5f-a8f3-154d30b790dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19777" :question/problem "Find $(2a+3c)\\times (b+2c)$, if $a\\times b=3i-2j+k$, $a\\times c=j-2k$ and $b\\times c=2i-3j.$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{c})\\times (\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) &= ((2\\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b})+((2\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}))+((3\\mathbf{c})\\times \\mathbf{b})+((3\\mathbf{c})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((2\\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b})+((2\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}))+((3\\mathbf{c})\\times \\mathbf{b})+((3\\mathbf{c})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})) &= 2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+(2\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})+3(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})+(3\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{c}) \\\\ &= 2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})+3(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})+6(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{c})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})+3(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b})+6(\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &= 2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})+6(\\mathbf{0}) \\\\ &= 2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})+4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &= 2(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+4(\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-3(2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}) \\\\ &= (6\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})+(4\\mathbf{j}-8\\mathbf{k})-(6\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}) \\\\ &= (6\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{i})+(-4\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{j}+9\\mathbf{j})+(2\\mathbf{k}-8\\mathbf{k}) \\\\ &= 9\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287957 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "034087b3-a93a-5e90-bae0-492e734813aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-19778" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨6,4,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 0-6\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 4-6\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0)\\mathbf{i}-(0)\\mathbf{j}+(8-18)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287958 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c7fa80b-0a29-596e-9b7c-a95cdd0f45ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-19782" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,0,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((0)(1)-(0)(3))\\mathbf{i}-((0)(1)-(2)(3))\\mathbf{j}+((0)(0)-(2)(0))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-0)\\mathbf{i}-(0-6)\\mathbf{j}+(0-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287959 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "421f4045-e8df-5b08-89fb-dae822bc3289" :question/math-academy-id "q-19785" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨x,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2x,5,2⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{393}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ x & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2x & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2 \\\\ 2x & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(4-15)\\mathbf{i}-(2x-6x)\\mathbf{j}+(5x-4x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-11,4x,x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{(-11)^{2}+(4x)^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{121+16{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ \\sqrt{17{x}^{2}+121} & =\\sqrt{393} \\\\ 17{x}^{2}+121 & =393 \\\\ 17{x}^{2} & =272 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\pm 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287960 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "07d09c7b-7392-52f3-9600-f879fb6f51f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-19787" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,5,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((5)(0)-(3)(4))\\mathbf{i}-((1)(0)-(2)(4))\\mathbf{j}+((1)(3)-(2)(5))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(0-12)\\mathbf{i}-(0-8)\\mathbf{j}+(3-10)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-12\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}-7\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287961 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0f6e61a-90ab-51e6-8143-08dbdea1e5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-19789" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,2,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((2)(-3)-(2)(0))\\mathbf{i}-((-2)(-3)-(0)(0))\\mathbf{j}+((-2)(2)-(0)(2))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(-6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6-0)\\mathbf{j}+(-4-0)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-6\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287979 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d7560d3-4e5c-58e1-a4ce-975d4218030d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20145" :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(2,1,2)$, $B(3,3,3)$, $C(-2,-1,3)$, and $D(4,1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-4\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-4,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+4\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)-2((-4)\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1)+((-4)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =-4+20+4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|20|=20.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287983 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76260352-1678-5f91-b36f-3214c8df1e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20263" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=6 \\\\ y+z &=5 \\\\ 2x+3y+2z &=14\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 & 14\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 & 14\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z &=6 \\\\ y+z &=5 \\\\ z &=3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. The final equation $z=3$ tells us the value of $z.$ Substituting this value into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(3)=5\\,⟹\\,y=5-3=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting both $y=2$ and $z=3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(2)+(3)=6\\,⟹\\,x=6-5=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=1,y=2$ and $z=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287997 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e202a5f2-1485-54d2-ad75-6c42b9340de9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20524" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6 & 21\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287998 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9aba0f5-8a1c-5403-81c5-fd64e80722c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-20533" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=0 \\\\ y+2z &=0 \\\\ x+y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287999 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00b50258-32a4-58d0-8a62-9857f481b38c" :question/math-academy-id "q-20542" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ x+2y+2z &=2 \\\\ 3x+4y+4z &=8\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 4 & 8\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 17\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 12\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=-3 \\\\ y &=5 \\\\ z &=-6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=5$ and $z=-6$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+5+2(-6)=-3\\,⟹\\,x-7=-3\\,⟹\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=4,$ $y=5,$ and $z=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288008 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64e30738-40d0-5a54-8cdd-1cc83ec4b628" :question/math-academy-id "q-20782" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 14 & 21\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 14 & 21\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y &=-3 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx-2y=-3.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-3+2y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3+2y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288009 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9e4d803-6c25-5024-8fca-536e9832cf45" :question/math-academy-id "q-20783" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ 2x+8y &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 4\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=2 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+4y=2.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=2-4y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-4y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288014 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "109305f2-78e5-5de6-8ad6-74eb1cf5626d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20845" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 & -2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=-1 \\\\ y+3z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-z &=-1 \\\\ y+3z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx-z=-1\\,⇒\\,x=-1+z\n$$\n\nand from the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\ny+3z=1\\,⇒\\,y=1-3z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+z \\\\ y &=1-3z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+z \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288020 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25615c72-6956-5e2b-84cd-a6850d4c806d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20958" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 28\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 28\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & -3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-3y &=7 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx-3y=7.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=7+3y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=7+3y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7+3y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288021 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b84d9fd-d3bf-5ad1-8468-147cba84321e" :question/math-academy-id "q-20960" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}8x+32y &=-40 \\\\ 5x+20y &=-25.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." :question/worked-solution "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}8 & 32 & -40 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}8 & 32 & -40 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 20 & -25\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+4y &=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx+4y=-5.\n$$\n\nThe first variable $(x)$ is the basic variable, and the second variable $(y)$ is the free variable. Solving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x=-5-4y,$ and the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5-4y \\\\ y &=y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ is an arbitrary number. \n\nIn vector form, the general solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5-4y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288022 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37df46aa-880b-52ef-a085-52132aef293d" :question/math-academy-id "q-20967" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+0y+2z &=-2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z &=3 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2z &=-2 \\\\ y-3z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3z &=3\\,⇒\\,y=3+3z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand from the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2z &=-2\\,⇒\\,x=-2-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2-2z \\\\ y &=3+3z \\\\ z &=z\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2-2z \\\\ 3+3z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+z\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288023 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22a37001-42ac-5755-9c7b-a4e97d0ac7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-20971" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\right].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 6 & 4\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+z &=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince any numbers $x,y,z$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just two equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2z &=1 \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ To do this, we apply the back substitution. \n\nStarting with the second equation $z=1,$ we substitute into the first equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2(1) &=1\\,⇒\\,x=-1-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1-y \\\\ y &=y \\\\ z &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-y \\\\ y \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+y\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290339 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0f3ebcb-819b-5060-93c1-2a4bba4f43b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-216349" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{a})+\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\times (2\\mathbf{a})+\\mathbf{a}\\times (3\\mathbf{b})=2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})+3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{a})+3(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) & =2(\\mathbf{0})+3(-\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =-3(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292832 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47f34483-7692-5e5a-8304-62b8ecc1d561" :question/math-academy-id "q-275275" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨6,-2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨5,0,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$\n\n$\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 0)\\mathbf{i}-(6\\cdot (-3)-0\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{j}+(6\\cdot 0-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(6-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-18-0)\\mathbf{j}+(0-(-10))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =6\\mathbf{i}+18\\mathbf{j}+10\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨6,18,10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292833 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c72f4aa-ab5b-5b58-b34b-802301025714" :question/math-academy-id "q-275278" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2x,3,5⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2x,0,1⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{909}.$ Then, in **ascending order**,  the possible values of $x$ are\n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -2x & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2x & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 5 \\\\ 2x & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-2x & 3 \\\\ 2x & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(-2x-10x)\\mathbf{j}+(0-6x)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,12x,-6x⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(12x)^{2}+(-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+144{x}^{2}+36{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ \\sqrt{180{x}^{2}+9} & =\\sqrt{909} \\\\ 180{x}^{2}+9 & =909 \\\\ 180{x}^{2} & =900 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\sqrt{5},$ $x=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292834 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64a063c4-201d-50ad-960d-a431962b0bcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-275297" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}=3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}.$ Find $(4\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}.$\n\n$(4\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{j}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n(4\\mathbf{a}-3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}=(4\\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{c}-(3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n(4\\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{c}-(3\\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c}=4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-3(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =4(3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-3(2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =12\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-6\\mathbf{i}-6\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =6\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292836 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48383069-88ac-53ba-a18f-1b9e7ee17931" :question/math-academy-id "q-275301" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}=2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}.$ Find $(6\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}).$\n\n$(6\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mathbf{i}\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\mathbf{j}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b})\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}\\pm \\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} \\\\ \\mathbf{c}\\times (\\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{b}) & =\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}\\pm \\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda \\mathbf{a})\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}) \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\times (\\lambda \\mathbf{b}) & =\\lambda (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n(6\\mathbf{a}-5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})=(6\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6\\mathbf{a})\\times (2\\mathbf{c})-(5\\mathbf{b})\\times (2\\mathbf{c}) & =(6\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-(5\\cdot 2)(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) \\\\  & =12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12(\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c})-10(\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =12(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j})-10(-4\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =24\\mathbf{i}-12\\mathbf{j}+40\\mathbf{i}-30\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =64\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-42\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292841 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4a8b31c-e171-5959-8851-e81cf194bca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-275353" :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,1,-3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-1,k,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-2,4⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & k & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & k \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2(k\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot k) \\\\  & =-8k-8+10-6+9k \\\\  & =k-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =0 \\\\ k-4 & =0 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292885 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30ba35b5-5f35-540d-9096-c30d7199aaa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-275755" :question/problem "Determine the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in the inverse of a matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{32}^{′}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2+2)-0-3(2-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{32}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{32}^{t}={c}_{23}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{23} & =(-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(6-0) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{32}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{32}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{23} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292886 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a90f7ea-2f5b-53dd-b349-fa9148f654dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-275756" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $det(A)=-k.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\ndet(A)=-k\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{12}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{12}^{t}={c}_{21}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{21} & =(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0+1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{12}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{12}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-k)}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{12}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{k} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292887 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b033d4e-fbed-55f2-bf1a-00675137d39e" :question/math-academy-id "q-275757" :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $-1.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the determinant of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2+k)-(-4+2)-(2k+2) \\\\  & =2+k+2-2k-2 \\\\  & =2-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want the entry ${a}_{13}^{′}$ in ${A}^{-1},$ we need to know the value of ${c}_{13}^{t}={c}_{31}$ in the matrix of cofactors. For this, we use the formula to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{31} & =(-1)^{3+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, since ${A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{13}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{13}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{det}(A)}{c}_{31} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-k}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{k-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are told that ${a}_{13}^{′}=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{k-2} & =-1 \\\\ -1\\cdot (k-2) & =2 \\\\ k-2 & =-2 \\\\ k & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292888 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291801} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bd4ffe0-c49d-548a-9828-1b36d2c269eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-275758" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+2y &=-1 \\\\ x-3y &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-2$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td><td>{{field-3}}</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$-1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292891 :question/answer-fields [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186291987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbf400af-4fb2-5200-ac54-d2206eb77cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-275763" :question/problem "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3y &=1-3x \\\\ x-2 &=-4y\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-answer\">\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">$\\mathbf{x}=$</span>\n<span class=\"matrix-answer-label\">{{field-1}}</span>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>{{field-2}}</td><td>$-3$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>{{field-3}}</td><td>$3$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n<div class=\"matrix-answer-brackets\">\n<table aria-label=\"Answer matrix\"><tbody>\n<tr><td>$1$</td></tr>\n<tr><td>$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+3y &=1 \\\\ x+4y &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4-1\\cdot 3}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{x} & ={A}^{-1}\\mathbf{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}]}
